## Introduction
Discoveries in inorganic and bioinorganic chemistry have had a significant impact on modern clinical medicine. These discoveries have predominantly emerged in the form of either metal-containing diagnostic imaging agents or metal-containing therapeutics [1]. In the area of medicinal imaging, arguably the most important contributions of bioinorganic chemistry are the use of gadolinium(III) complexes as magnetic resonance imaging contrast agents (MRI-CA; e.g. Dotarem, Figure 1a) [2] and $^{99}\mathrm{Tc}$ complexes in cardiovascular imaging (e.g. Cardiolyte, Figure 1a) [3]. In the realm of metal-containing therapeutics, the most prominent discovery is that of cisplatin (cis-diamminedichloroplatinum(II), cis-DDP) [4], which is one of the most widely employed anticancer drugs (Figure 1b). The success of cisplatin can be considered particularly notable because: (i) the drug is truly inorganic, containing no carbon atoms (although the clinically used analog carboplatin does contain carbon), and (ii) the compound has effectively cured at least one type of cancer (testicular cancer). In addition to cisplatin, other metal-dependent therapeutics have seen varying degrees of clinical success, including compounds containing gold (e.g. Auranofin, antiarthritics) [5], lithium (e.g. lithium carbonate, antidepressants) [6], and iron (e.g. Bleomycin, anticancer) [7]. In 1999, a thematic issue of Chemical Reviews entitled ‘Medicinal Inorganic Chemistry’ provided an extensive review of many subjects in this field of study [8].
In addition to the clinically approved uses of metalcontaining compounds described above, numerous investigations have been performed and are ongoing for identifying new medicinal agents from throughout the periodic table. Isotopes of copper, gallium, indium and ytterbium have all been examined for radiolabel imaging [9], and manganese and iron compounds have been investigated for use as MRI-CA. Vanadium compounds have been extensively studied for use as insulin mimetics for treating diabetes [10], whereas metallocene complexes (primarily of titanium) and dinuclear rhodium complexes have been investigated as chemotherapeutics for diseases such as cancer [11]. Conversely, the investigation of metal chelators, for removal of toxic metal ions or for binding to the active site of metalloproteins (thereby inhibiting their activity) has been another active area of inorganic chemistry with medical relevance [12,13].
In perhaps a less obvious manner than the development of metal-containing or metal-chelating medicinal agents, bioinorganic chemistry has also made important contributions to medicine by elucidating the mechanism of diseases wherein the underlying biology is associated with metalloproteins or metal ion homeostasis. Some of the diseases in which metal ions or a metalloprotein have been strongly implicated are Wilson and Menkes disease (Wilson and Menkes disease proteins; copper) [14], amytrophic lateral sclerosis (also known as Lou Gehrig’s disease; $\mathrm{{Cu/Zn}}$ superoxide dismutase; copper) [15], Alzheimer’s disease ( $\mathrm{.}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm{|\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm{|}\mathrm\mat