## Organometallic scaffolds for drug development
Metals ions not only possess much of the interesting reactivity and spectroscopy within the periodic table, but they also engage in some of the most diverse binding modes and geometries among the elements. Carbon, the most important element in drug design, can generate inert, covalent bonds with connectivities of two to four, and with bonding geometries about the carbon atom that range from linear to trigonal planar to tetrahedral. This has nevertheless allowed for an incredible number of different molecules to be prepared based on carbon, despite the fact that the types of bonding geometries obtainable are fairly limited. By contrast, metal ions can create either labile or inert bonds with coordination numbers ranging from one to twelve, and with numerous geometries including linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, octahedral and many others. This simple comparison suggests that metal ions can be used as new scaffolds about which one can construct therapeutic molecules that achieve shapes and structures that would be impossible or extremely difficult to achieve with carbon-based compounds. Of course, the converse could be argued as well, but considering the dearth of studies on metal-based pharmaceuticals (termed ‘metallopharmaceuticals’) it is prudent to investigate whether metals can be exploited in this fashion. The concept of metallopharmaceuticals as protein inhibitors has been previously explored [17], but some recent findings have sparked renewed interest in this exciting area of research.
In several reports, Meggers and co-workers $[18^{\bullet\bullet},19-22]$ provide strong evidence that the construction of therapeutics about a metal ion is indeed viable. The focus of these studies has been the natural product staurosporine (Figure 2), a potent, broad-spectrum inhibitor of protein kinases. Protein kinases represent an important class of drug targets, as they are involved in a multitude of cellular regulatory functions. By examining the 3D structure of staurosporine, Meggers and colleagues recognized that a metal-based fragment containing a bisbenzimidazolomaleimide (BBIM) ligand could readily reproduce the indolocarbazole alkaloid scaffold. Furthermore, use of such a metallocene compound would allow for a modular approach to access numerous compounds by simply changing the ligands bound to the metal center $\mathrm{(L_{1}\mathrm{-}L_{4}}$ , see Figure 2). Initial studies generated a modest set of kinase inhibitors based on ruthenium(II). The complexes displayed evidence for competitive inhibition, indicating that the metallopharmaceuticals were binding at the intended ATP-binding site within the kinases [20].
Soon after this study, the metallopharmaceutical approach was clearly validated by the identification of a ruthenium(II) complex as a nanomolar inhibitor of glycogen synthase kinase 3 (GSK-3) [19]. The rutheniu$\mathrm{m}(\mathrm{II})$ complex, which contained a pyridocarbazole ligand (PC, Figure 2), was stable in aqueous media, demonstrated good selectivity over other kinases, and was actually more potent than staurosporine against GSK-3. The metal and additional ancillary ligands were proven to be essential for good activity as the pyridocarbazole ligand alone was a poor inhibitor of GSK-3. The crystal structure of a protected precursor to the ruthenium(II)–pyridocarbazole complex was determined and used to show the structural similarity to staurosporine and predict the mode of binding in the GSK-3 active site. Since these first reports, the utility and versatility of these metal-based kinase inhibitors have been further developed. Biological studies have shown that some ruthenium(II) derivatives can activate gene pathways in living Xenopus embryos by inhibiting GSK-3 [22]. Additional synthetic studies have shown that by screening ligands bound to the rutheniu$\mathrm{m}(\mathrm{II})$ -pyridocarbazole fragment, inhibitors of at least three different protein kinases could be found $[18^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . Finally, the structure of two ruthenium(II) complexes bound to the protein kinase Pim-1 (Figure 3) have been solved $[23^{\bullet}]$ . These exciting new findings in metallopharmaceuticals are sure to catch the interest and imagination of bioinorganic and medicinal chemists alike.
## Inorganic materials in medicine
A growing area of solid-state chemistry involves the synthesis of hybrid inorganic–organic compounds often referred to as metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). MOFs are microporous, low-density crystalline solids generally comprised of polytopic organic ligand linkers and metal ion or metal ion cluster nodes $\left[24,25^{\bullet}\right]$ . Interest in MOFs has seen explosive growth in the past decade, with an enormous number of compounds prepared containing metal ions from throughout the periodic table. The major interest in MOFs has been in the area of high-density gas storage for potential use in separations, fuel cells and other energy-related technologies. However, two recent
