reports suggest that these materials might play an exciting new role in medical imaging and drug delivery. In the first study, Lin and co-workers $[26^{\bullet\bullet}]$ described the synthesis of known MOFs constructed from lanthanide ions and carboxylate linkers in a nanoparticle morphology, as opposed to the macroscopic crystalline material that is typically achieved in MOF synthesis. By selecting lanthanide ions as the metal nodes, the study showed that these nanoparticle MOFs (NMOFs) could be dispersed in solution and used as potential imaging agents. NMOFs based on gadolinium(III) were shown to have very high relaxivity, the parameter which characterizes efficient MRI-CA. Nanorods $(100\times40~\mathrm{nm})$ of the NMOF had a measured relaxivity $(R_{1})$ of $35.8~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ per mM of gadolinium(III) $[26^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . This exceptionally high relaxivity exceeds those of other macromolecular gadolinium(III) agents, such as gadolinium(III)-containing liposomes. Doping of the gadolinium(III) NMOFs with $5\%$ terbiu$\mathrm{m}(\mathrm{III})$ or $5\%$ europium(III) generated particles with both high relaxivity and an intense luminescence characteristic of the doping ion (green for terbium, red for europium).
Generalized scheme for the synthesis and use of MOFs (MIL-101) as drug delivery vehicles.
The doped NMOFs were proposed as a new class of multimodal imaging agents, in which both magnetic resonance and luminescence could be utilized simultaneously. Although several questions will need to be answered for NMOFs (e.g. aqueous stability and biocompatibility) to further validate this proof-of-concept report, it is a highly novel approach in the area of metals in medicine. With several other recent reports that describe other synthetic routes to NMOFs [27,28], this area seems poised to provide some exciting results in the coming years.
In the second study, the high porosity of MOFs has been exploited for use as a drug delivery vehicle. Perhaps as important as the development of new therapeutics is the ability to deliver drugs locally as opposed to systemically in a patient. The ability to engineer controlled, localized delivery of a potentially toxic molecule might contribute significantly to both the efficiency of the treatment as well as reducing undesired side effects. Because MOFs are highly porous, low-density materials, they are well known for their ability to uptake guest molecules within the channels of the framework. Although in many MOFs these channels are only large enough to harbor small organic molecules such as solvents and gases, some MOFs have been described with extremely large pore volumes. For example, MOF-177 from the laboratory of Yaghi and co-workers $[29^{\bullet}]$ was shown to have a pore volume of $1.59\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and was capable of absorbing large dye molecules such as Astrazon Orange R, Nile Red, Reichardt’s dye, and even $\mathrm{C}_{60}!$ With this in mind, Fe´ rey and coworkers [30,31] took chromium-carboxylate MOFs termed MIL-100 and MIL-101 (MIL, Materials of Institut Lavoisier) and successfully loaded the anti-inflammatory drug Ibuprofen into the pores (Figure 4) $[32^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . High loadings of Ibuprofen were achieved, with $0.35~\mathrm{g}$ and $1.4\mathrm{g}$ per gram of MOF, for MIL-100 and MIL-101, respectively. Even at these high loadings of guest, no apparent loss of crystallinity or other decomposition of the framework structure was observed (as evidenced by X-ray powder diffraction). The kinetics of Ibuprofen release were monitored by using HPLC into a simulated body fluid solution; complete guest release was achieved in approximately three and six days from MIL-100 and MIL-101, respectively. Again, much like the reported use of MOFs in imaging, the use of MOFs for drug delivery is still in its infancy, but the proof-of-principle has now been reported and one can expect many more exciting results to follow. Indeed, the use of NMOFs as a drug delivery vehicle has not yet been described, but one would anticipate these experiments to be reported soon.
## Conclusions
Compounds containing metal ions have and will continue to play an important role in biomedical technology. Whether as imaging agents, therapeutics or probes for chemical genetics, inorganic compounds will continue to provide the research community with tools that cannot be achieved with organic chemistry alone. By highlighting just a few discoveries that seem poised to grow significantly within the area of medicinal bioinorganic chemistry, it is hoped that a new generation of scientists will be inspired to take part in the exciting discoveries that lay ahead.
